# Cargo.lock IS TRACKED in this fork — a deliberate divergence from upstream,
# which gitignores it (their listed reasons: dependency churn, `cargo install
# sqlx-cli` picking latest deps unless --locked, "libraries don't really need
# reproducible builds"). The fork's fleet CI invokes cargo with --locked
# everywhere (the rust-service lanes, and tests/ci-cheap.sh / ci-verify.sh),
# which refuses to create a missing lock file — measured: "cannot create the
# lock file ... because --locked was passed" — and the seven published
# crates' registry bytes resolve from exactly this lock. The fork's
# lifecycle is publish-once, then drop when upstream releases both pool
# bounds, so the churn upstream avoids cannot occur here.